From a7a32e421c4eca96459da0015e720d71efc370a3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Quintin Smits <qd.smits@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 14:55:38 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] feat(wire): arcs on model-based wire paths

A Wire Contour of a meshed model (the through-silhouette or a picked
model edge) reads its one closed profile back as lines and arcs: a run
of at least 3 facet chords whose vertices lie within 1 um of one circle,
each chord bowing no more than the model's chord tolerance, is that
circle's arc. Split points on a chord are dropped first; fillet ends
are made tangent to their straight sides. The chain is cut by the
analytic wire contour of #3937, so the generic ISO, Sodick and G-EDM
posts write G2 / G3 in place of G01 chords. A profile with no arc keeps
its polygon cut byte for byte; skims, tabs, burnout, exit pick, taper
and 4-axis keep the polygon path.

The wire verifier judges such a cut under the model's chord tolerance
plus the fit tolerance (the arcs pass between the facet chords and the
true circle) and says so on the verdict line; the undercut law still
runs only for a taper.

Fixes #3215

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
